  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Search Open Websites & Domains — Search Engines", "technique": "T1593.002", "what_happened": "At 2026-02-09 10:14:03 CET, Suricata + the Wazuh web-access decoder logged the first of a tightly-sequenced burst of HTTP GET requests from data.srcip=203.0.113.66 to data.dstip=10.108.57.177 (corporate webserver). The requested data.url values were NOT crawl-pattern paths (no /, /index.html, /favicon.ico, /robots.txt walk) — they were a curated list of deep, specific, non-linked sensitive paths: /internal/finance-2024-Q4-budget.pdf, /internal/security-policy.pdf, /docs/network-topology.pdf, /downloads/employee-handbook.pdf. A path set this specific is NEVER discovered by an on-site crawler — it is the output of Google-dork queries (site:corp.local filetype:pdf inurl:internal) run BEFORE touching the target. Step 1 is the attacker probing the document paths their dorking surfaced.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh web rule fired on data.http.method=GET from a single external data.srcip requesting >=4 distinct deep data.url paths under /internal/ and /docs/ within 60 seconds — none of which are reachable by following links from the site root. The data.http.user_agent was a static desktop Chrome string identical across every request (no browser would request 12 unlinked deep paths back-to-back). rule.mitre.id T1593.002 (Search Open Websites/Domains: Search Engines) attached because the path list is the signature of search-engine-indexed-document harvesting.", "first_seen": "2026-02-09T10:14:03+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Search Open Websites & Domains — Sensitive File Probing", "technique": "T1593.002", "what_happened": "Between 10:15:00 and 10:16:00 CET the same data.srcip=203.0.113.66 escalated from document paths to data-exposure paths: /static/staff-directory.xlsx, /uploads/legacy-export.zip, /admin/db-backup-2024.sql.gz, /backup/archive-old.tar.gz. This is the attacker walking from 'indexed PDFs' to 'is the database backup also exposed?' — the classic dork-probing escalation where each successful 200 OK encourages the next, more sensitive guess. The /admin/db-backup-2024.sql.gz and /backup/archive-old.tar.gz requests are the high-value targets: a single exposed SQL dump is a full-database compromise with zero exploitation needed.", "chain_reason": "Same data.srcip + same data.http.user_agent + same data.dstip continuing the GET sequence into paths matching the sensitive-archive pattern (*.sql.gz, *.tar.gz, *.zip, *.xlsx under /admin/, /backup/, /uploads/). The Wazuh rule correlated these into the same incident as Step 1 because the source, target, user-agent and 14-second inter-request cadence were identical.", "first_seen": "2026-02-09T10:15:02+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Search Open Websites & Domains — Secrets & Staging Probing", "technique": "T1593.002", "what_happened": "Between 10:16:00 and 10:17:01 CET the same data.srcip=203.0.113.66 finished the sequence with the highest-sensitivity guesses: /test-server/index.php, /private/.htaccess.bak, /old/login-test.html, /staging/api-keys.txt. The /staging/api-keys.txt and /private/.htaccess.bak requests are direct attempts at credential / secret retrieval; /test-server/ and /old/ are forgotten-asset probing (staging and legacy directories that are commonly left exposed and unpatched). This is the terminal phase of dork-driven probing: after documents and backups, go for the secrets and the forgotten dev assets.", "chain_reason": "Same data.srcip + same data.http.user_agent continuing into paths matching the secrets/staging pattern (*api-keys*, *.htaccess.bak, /staging/, /test-server/, /old/). Wazuh kept all 12 requests in one correlated alert: one external source, one webserver target, one user-agent, 12 unlinked deep paths, ~3 minute total window, strict 14-second cadence between requests = scripted dork-path probing, not human browsing.", "first_seen": "2026-02-09T10:16:01+0100"}
